          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.11,
            0.881,
            0.168
          ],
          "content": "**Proof of (c):** Call the intersection point of the two diagonals E. Using property (a), $\\Delta  AED \\cong \\Delta  CEB$ , by ASA or AAS. So EA = EC and EB = ED, which means that E is the midpoint of both diagonals."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.168,
            0.883,
            0.303
          ],
          "content": "We will now deduce the converse statements. To prove the converse of a known theorem, we can try to run the original proof backwards. This strategy may or may not work. (For example, the reasoning $\\text{\" If } x = y_{:}$ , then $x ^ { 2 } = y ^ { 2 }$ cannot be reversed, because x and y may have opposite signs, e.g., we could have x = 3 and y = –3.) When the strategy does work, we should be careful to give proper justification for each step in the backward direction."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.305,
            0.882,
            0.345
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 2 (converse of (a)):** If the opposite sides of a quadrilateral are of equal length, then it is a parallelogram."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.4,
            0.348,
            0.62,
            0.455
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.379,
                0.462,
                0.619,
                0.48
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 12.7: Converse to (a)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.496,
            0.881,
            0.555
          ],
          "content": "**Proof:** Let ABCD be this quadrilateral. $\\Delta  ACD \\cong \\Delta  CAB$ by SSS, so corresponding angles are equal. Using transversal AC, we get AB ‖DC and AD ‖BC."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.559,
            0.883,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": "Pay close attention to how the above proof retraces the proof of (a) backwards. Both proofs go via the same congruence $\\Delta  ACD \\cong \\Delta  CAB ,$ but the two proofs use different tests to justify the congruence (ASA vs SSS) and draw different conclusions. Try to use the same ‘backward retracingʼ strategy for the remaining converses."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.66,
            0.881,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 3 (converse of (b)):** If the opposite angles of a quadrilateral are equal, then it is a parallelogram."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.705,
            0.879,
            0.743
          ],
          "content": "**Proof:** Let ABCD be this quadrilateral. Because $\\angle \\mathrm{A} = \\angle \\mathrm{C}$ and $\\angle \\mathrm{B} = \\angle \\mathrm{D}$ we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.214,
            0.749,
            0.781,
            0.768
          ],
          "content": "360^{\\circ} \\mp \\angle \\mathrm{A} + \\angle \\mathrm{B} + \\angle \\mathrm{C} + \\angle \\mathrm{D} = 2(\\angle \\mathrm{A} + \\angle \\mathrm{B}) = 2(\\angle \\mathrm{B} + \\angle \\mathrm{C}).",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.394,
            0.774,
            0.607,
            0.886
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.38,
                0.889,
                0.618,
                0.907
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 12.8: Converse to (b)"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.429,
